# keur quality control — bash script without strict mode (`set -euo pipefail`).
#
# Why this exists: the consumer repo `security-tooling` runs a CI check
# (check-strict-mode) that fails a bash script lacking `set -euo pipefail`.
# Without strict mode an unset variable expands to empty, a failed command in
# the middle of a script is ignored, and a failure in a pipeline is masked by
# the exit status of the last stage — three silent-failure classes. Porting the
# norm into keur gives every repo the same local pre-commit nudge, as a SECOND
# net (the consumer's CI keeps its own dependency-free check; keur does not
# replace it). @see .tmp/enrich-prompt-ci-checks-to-rules.md (Group 1).
#
# HOW (absence engine): the rule fires when NONE of the target's lines match the
# strict-mode pattern. `set -euo pipefail` is matched flexibly (any order of the
# e/u/o flags is common), anchored to a line so a mention in a comment/string is
# less likely to satisfy it by accident.
#
# WARN, not block: a missing strict-mode line is a robustness smell, not a bug,
# and some scripts deliberately manage errors by hand. Scope: bash/sh scripts;
# tests and vendored/build dirs excluded. @see AGENTS.md, docs/rules-authoring.md.
id: KEUR-QUAL-011
ilities: [analysability]
maturity: 3
title: Bash script without strict mode (set -euo pipefail)
category: quality
severity: warning
engine: absence
action: warn
confidence: 0.6
target:
  extensions: .sh .bash
  exclude_paths: test/ tests/ vendor/ build/ dist/
patterns:
  # Match a `set` line enabling errexit + nounset + pipefail in any flag order.
  # absence engine fires when this pattern appears NOWHERE in the file.
  - regex: (?m)^\s*set\s+-[a-z]*e[a-z]*\s+.*pipefail|^\s*set\s+-euo\s+pipefail
    message: "Bash script has no `set -euo pipefail` — unset vars, mid-script failures and broken pipes pass silently. Add strict mode near the top. @see KEUR-QUAL-011"
fix: "Add `set -euo pipefail` just after the shebang (and consider `IFS=$'\\n\\t'`). If a script genuinely needs to continue past failures, scope that with explicit `|| true` / `set +e` blocks rather than dropping strict mode entirely."
